  "ownership_design": {
    "actual_raw_handoff": "Before relinquishing an incremental live lease, abandon_failed removes the private pointer and clears initialized bits. It then invokes the existing allocator handoff exactly once for nonzero typed layouts, drops the private allocator handle and resumes the original panic. No payload access or deallocation follows; resetting raw storage cannot invalidate a Rust reference.",
    "completed_payloads": "Completed child payload destructors are intentionally not invoked. Failed in-progress node construction unwinds its already-constructed fields naturally. Native default failures and later assignments are tested separately, including partially assigned payload values retained in abandoned bytes.",
    "broader_owned_domain": "TreePairAllocation::new_with retains its prior cleanup policy and original-panic precedence; only new_native_with/edit_native select raw abandonment. No public type layout, Send/Sync implementation or borrow bound changes.",
    "zst": "Zero-sized pair owners never acquired an allocator lease and therefore do not release one. A regression holds an unrelated typed allocation while failing a zero-sized pair and verifies reset still rejects the live unrelated allocation.",
    "shared_helpers": "No changes to allocator/owned.rs or its lease machinery. The integrated typed-to-raw handoff and unsafe typed re-adoption API are reused, with their original policy/layout contract.",
    "test_only_repair": "A warmed scalar-aligned Acc fixture captures authentic zero-byte cursor provenance, balances its raw count, and computes the next pair address by downward offset within the existing backing block. Successful paired allocation controls verify exact pointer identity. Failed cases re-adopt MaybeUninit<[Node;2]> with the original exact typed layout, drop only known completed slots, never follow leaked links, and free once. Private-owner tests instead retain the original pointer directly and cover all five allocator policies. No production repair API or synthetic allocation addresses were introduced."
  },
  "native_observations": [
    "16 new rows distinguish bound/object default failure in first/second child and bound/object assignment failure in first/second assignment, for a leaf parent and an existing branch parent. Each row includes failed callback trace, real outstanding allocation count, explicit Clear trace/count, then fixture-only repair destructor trace and final count0.",
    "NB1/NO1 retain no completed child, NB2/NO2 retain the first complete child, and assignment failures retain two children with their precise partially assigned fields. The failed object constructor still destroys its already-constructed bounds. No automatic cleanup of completed siblings occurs.",
    "Native Clear releases only reachable tree nodes, leaving the unpublished pair outstanding. Repair explicitly destroys only fully constructed nodes, then frees their actual pair. This repair is fixture behavior, not a claim that native Clear reclaims its leaked pair.",
    "All16 previously partial prepublication Gemmate matrix rows, three borrowed UBTree Add rows, and six EBTree operation/Clear rows now compare exact ordinary native callback/state/allocation behavior. Successful published-pair destruction behavior remains unchanged.",
    "Six native noexcept hash/equality subprocess observations remain exact pretermination snapshots only. Rust caught-panic recovery remains a separate behavior and this ownership correction makes no termination parity claim."
  ],
  "native_row_test_bindings": {
    "retained0/1 NB1/NB2/NO1/NO2/AB1/AB2/AO1/AO2": "ubtree_failed_pair_contracts::retained_pair_and_test_only_repair_match_all_native_failure_phases",
